ACTION REQUIRED (engineering note — not itself part of the license terms
above; delete this section once a final license is in place):

"All rights reserved" was chosen deliberately as the placeholder default
because it is the strictest and legally safest position available without
committing to final terms — it blocks redistribution/sublicensing by
default until someone with legal authority decides otherwise, rather than
accidentally under-protecting the codebase. Before any commercial release,
sale, or distribution outside the organization, replace this file with a
final license reviewed by whoever owns legal/licensing decisions, covering
at minimum:

  1. Distribution model: does an end customer receive a EULA (a commercial
     license granting specific, limited rights to run the software), or
     does the code stay fully closed with a service/appliance model (the
     customer receives a sealed AI Box and never touches source or
     binaries directly)? This materially changes what the LICENSE needs to
     say.

  2. Third-party dependency license compliance — in particular
     `ultralytics` (the YOLO26 training/export library pinned in
     requirements.txt), which ships under AGPL-3.0 by default. Confirm
     whether this project's actual usage (offline model export vs. any
     runtime linkage) requires an Ultralytics Enterprise License before
     commercial distribution — this is a real compliance risk, not a
     formality. See README.md § "Third-party licenses" for the full list
     of dependencies to review.

  3. Data protection / privacy terms for face-recognition (biometric) data
     — regulations vary significantly by jurisdiction (GDPR in the EU,
     Vietnam's Personal Data Protection Decree, BIPA-style biometric laws
     in parts of the US, etc.) and by deployment sector (healthcare/elder
     care carries additional obligations in most jurisdictions). This
     belongs in a separate Data Processing Agreement / privacy policy, not
     necessarily in LICENSE itself, but must exist before commercial sale.

  4. Patent grant / indemnification terms, warranty/liability limits
     appropriate for a product deployed in elder-care and safety-critical
     contexts (fall detection, zone violation alerting) — a technical team
     should not finalize these without counsel given the liability profile.
